<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · island</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kyushu</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Kyūshū</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kyushu is the third-largest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>'s main islands, lying south-west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/honshu">Honshu</a> and closest to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. It was an early gateway for continental culture and, in the 16th century, for Portuguese traders and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>. Its great warrior houses, such as the Shimazu, Ōtomo and Ryūzōji, fought for control until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toyotomi-hideyoshi">Toyotomi Hideyoshi</a> conquered the island in 1587.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kyushu.jpg" alt="Kyushu" width="320" height="345" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: NASA · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Terra_Kyushu_20091028.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
